sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS R1-R4 + 2 (DNS hold), firewall.allow shape, Gateway signs CSRs (root-of-trust), single-swamp-node. docs/cli/route.mdx, docs/reference/routes.md.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: route:create
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane V icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant N as Route Nodes<br/>workload · Router
  participant V as VPN Node<br/>private DNS
  CLI->>GW: route:create 12 shop.test<br/>--publication=private
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit route:create 12 shop.test --publication=private`
  %%> - syntax: `<instance> <domain> [--publication=private|public] [--web-root=PATH]`
  %%> Other forms:
  %%> - custom proxy: `<domain> --node=NODE --upstream=URL` or `<domain> --node=NODE --process=PROCESS`
  %%> - public Process Route: `<domain> --process=PROCESS [--ingress=NODE] --publication=public`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - Instance 12 sets the Project and the scope: its Node, or its active Cluster
  %%> - the domain; the Gateway normalizes it
  %%> - publication is `private` unless you ask for `public`
  %%> Who can call it:
  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request
  Note over GW: Validate, reserve domain<br/>Route 14 pending · task #5120
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 7, the Node of Instance 12
  %%> - Instance 12 is active and has a supported web root
  %%> - the domain is valid, free, and not a reserved name such as `gateway.orbit`
  %%> - Instance 12 has no other Route without a web root (`route.target_conflict`)
  %%> - a Cluster-scoped Route needs an active Router (`route.router_required`)
  %%> - a public Route needs production targets and a serving Ingress
  %%> Locks:
  %%> - the projection lock and the Instance environment owner of Instance 12
  %%> - a busy lock is waited for up to 30 s, then `app-dev.projection_busy`
  %%> Builds:
  %%> - the Caddy site blocks for every affected Node, the certificate leaves, the LAN tuples and the private DNS records
  %%> Result:
  %%> - Route 14 recorded as `pending`, unpublished, with the domain reserved
  %%> - task #5120 recorded with the full intent
  %%> - nothing has happened on a Node yet
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-route-create
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - the rendered Caddy blocks before and after, per Node
  %%> - the certificate leaves: Node, scope, domain, Node user
  %%> - LAN tuples, DNS records, probes, and each Node's address and SSH host keys
  %%> - production `.env` material only as vault key names in `orbit-control-inputs` (R3)
  %%> - the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5120
  %%> What you get:
  %%> - the task number, at once
  %%> Next:
  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`
  opt development target
    SW->>N: Prepare the web root<br/>setfacl as the Node user
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - denies Caddy the rest of the checkout and its Git directory
    %%> - grants Caddy read access to the web root, and passage through the directories above it
    %%> - converges the shared PHP-FPM pool for Instance 12
    %%> Runs as:
    %%> - the Node's managed user; root only above every checkout
    %%> When:
    %%> - the target is a development Instance; production releases already have access
  end
  SW->>N: Make a CSR on the Node<br/>openssl req · key stays on Node
  %%> How:
  %%> - Swamp reaches Node 7, and the Router for a Cluster Route, over `@swamp/ssh` from 209
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - makes a new private key and a certificate request for `shop.test`
  %%> - returns only the public CSR to Swamp
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the private key never leaves the Node
  SW->>GW: Sign the CSR<br/>operation:direct:certificate-sign
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - sends the CSRs with task #5120 and the run id
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the Orbit CA key stays in the Gateway; signing is the one step that calls the Gateway
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the Gateway checks the task, the run, and that each CSR matches an admitted leaf
  GW-->>SW: Signed certificate<br/>Orbit CA key stays in Gateway
  %%> Returns:
  %%> - one public certificate bundle per leaf
  SW->>N: Install the certificate<br/>/etc/caddy/orbit-certificates
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - writes the certificate next to its key, owned `root:caddy`, mode 0750
  %%> - reads it back and checks it matches the key and the domain
  opt Cluster-scoped Route
    SW->>N: Allow the Router on LAN<br/>ufw allow 443 from Router LAN
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - on the workload Node, allows TCP 443 from the Router's LAN address
    %%> - an identical tuple that another Route uses is left as it is
    %%> Why:
    %%> - the Router forwards to the workload over LAN, with no WireGuard fallback
  end
  SW->>N: Publish the Caddy site<br/>caddy validate, then reload
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - writes the new site blocks into the Caddyfile of each affected Node
  %%> - validates the whole file, then reloads Caddy
  %%> Why reload:
  %%> - a reload keeps open connections; Caddy is never restarted (rule 7)
  %%> Refuses:
  %%> - a validation error; the old Caddyfile stays in place
  SW->>N: Probe the site over TLS<br/>openssl s_client · SNI shop.test
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - connects from 209 to the workload, or the Router, on port 443 with the Route domain as SNI
  %%> - requires a chain the Orbit root trusts
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the Route only counts as served when the probe passes
  %%> Not included:
  %%> - the application's own HTTP answer; a 500 does not fail the Route
  opt public Route
    SW->>N: Open the Ingress edge<br/>Ingress Caddy · ufw 80/443
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - publishes the public site on the Ingress, with `tls force_automate` for Let's Encrypt
    %%> - opens `orbit:ingress-http` and `orbit:ingress-https` when they are closed
    %%> Not included:
    %%> - the public DNS record; you create it yourself
    %%> - waiting for Let's Encrypt; Caddy retries on its own
  end
  SW->>V: Publish private DNS<br/>records + catalog, then reload
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - adds `shop.test`, pointing at Node 7, or at the Router for a Cluster Route
  %%> - checks the loaded catalog and a DNS reply
  %%> Order:
  %%> - DNS goes last, so a name only resolves when its site serves
  Note over V: open: Nick decides<br/>DNS hold · native-dns-takeover
  %%> Open:
  %%> - DNS publication is under the DNS hold; Nick decides (rule 9)
  %%> - until then, the run skips this step and Route work stays same-Node work
  alt every step succeeds
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event + outputs
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Record result<br/>Route 14 active · task succeeded
    %%> Result:
    %%> - Route 14 becomes `active` and Instance 12 shows `https://shop.test`
    %%> - task #5120 succeeds
  else a step fails
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
    %%> The Gateway reads:
    %%> - which step failed and its error code
    Note over GW: Mark the Route failed<br/>domain kept · rerun after a fix
    %%> Result:
    %%> - Route 14 is marked `failed` with `failed_step` and `error_code`; the domain stays reserved
    %%> - task #5120 fails
    %%> On the Node:
    %%> - what the run added stays; there is no rollback
    %%> - that is safe: no private DNS name points at the new site yet, and the record shows `failed`
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - run the same command again after fixing the cause; it is a new task and skips finished steps
    %%> - to give up instead, run `route:destroy 14`; it removes what the run added
  end
  loop every 2 s until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5120
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended
    GW-->>CLI: status + result
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended
  end
